<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>

  <!--
    Package sources for this solution. <clear /> drops any inherited sources
    from machine-level NuGet.Config so the build is reproducible from this
    file alone.

    Deliberately no `github` source and no packageSourceCredentials block.
    This project consumes BigRational by ProjectReference, not by package, so
    the GITHUB_PACKAGES_PAT the other members need would be a restore failure
    mode here with nothing behind it. Add the source back only if a
    HalHeinrich.* package is ever referenced from this repo.
  -->
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
  </packageSources>

  <!--
    Deliberately no packageSourceMapping. It is not omitted by oversight and
    should not be added to match the members that carry one.

    The `*` mapping removed here was described as "the guard that stays
    correct if a second source is ever added". That is backwards. With one
    declared source it is inert for every package this repo restores: mapping
    `*` to nuget.org selects exactly the resolution no mapping at all
    produces. Its only effect would arrive on the day a second source is
    added, and measured on SDK 10.0.400 that effect points the wrong way. With
    a mapping present NuGet considers only the sources whose patterns match a
    given package id and names the rest as "not considered" in the restore
    error, so a `*` mapped to nuget.org would exclude a newly added github
    feed from every package, the HalHeinrich.* one that motivated adding the
    feed included. The private name would then be sought on the public feed
    and nowhere else, which is the direction dependency confusion runs rather
    than a defence against it.

    Dropped rather than corrected. Both remove the false claim; only dropping
    leaves the file without a construct whose sole remaining purpose would be
    to warn about itself.

    Note this repo does depend on HalHeinrich.Numerics.BigRational, by the
    ProjectReference named above - that is what keeps it single-source, and it
    is the condition to watch rather than a permanent property. If that edge
    ever becomes a PackageReference, add the github source above and, in the
    same change, a packageSourceMapping section mapping `HalHeinrich.*` to
    github and `*` to nuget.org. Both patterns: the second is not optional,
    because the mapping that names the first excludes every source it does not
    name.
  -->

</configuration>
